@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2026 = private unnamed_addr constant [854 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for type alias definitions that are not annotated with\0A`typing.TypeAlias`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, a type alias is defined by assigning a type to a variable (e.g.,\0A`Vector = list[float]`).\0A\0AIt's best to annotate type aliases with the `typing.TypeAlias` type to\0Amake it clear that the statement is a type alias declaration, as opposed\0Ato a normal variable assignment.\0A\0A## Example\0A```pyi\0AVector = list[float]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```pyi\0Afrom typing import TypeAlias\0A\0AVector: TypeAlias = list[float]\0A```\0A\0A## Availability\0A\0ABecause this rule relies on the third-party `typing_extensions` module for Python versions\0Abefore 3.10, its diagnostic will not be emitted, and no fix will be offered, if\0A`typing_extensions` imports have been disabled by the [`lint.typing-extensions`] linter option.\0A\0A## Options\0A\0A- `lint.typing-extensions`\0A", align 1
