@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2072 = private unnamed_addr constant [1055 x i8] c"## Removal\0AThis rule has been removed because marking fixtures that do not return a value with an underscore\0Aisn't a practice recommended by the pytest community.\0A\0A## What it does\0AChecks for `pytest` fixtures that do not return a value, but are not named\0Awith a leading underscore.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABy convention, fixtures that don't return a value should be named with a\0Aleading underscore, while fixtures that do return a value should not.\0A\0AThis rule ignores abstract fixtures and generators.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture()\0Adef patch_something(mocker):\0A    mocker.patch(\22module.object\22)\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture()\0Adef use_context():\0A    with create_context():\0A        yield\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture()\0Adef _patch_something(mocker):\0A    mocker.patch(\22module.object\22)\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture()\0Adef _use_context():\0A    with create_context():\0A        yield\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [`pytest` documentation: `@pytest.fixture` functions](https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/reference.html#pytest-fixture)\0A", align 1
@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2073 = private unnamed_addr constant [1039 x i8] c"## Removal\0AThis rule has been removed because marking fixtures that do not return a value with an underscore\0Aisn't a practice recommended by the pytest community.\0A\0A## What it does\0AChecks for `pytest` fixtures that return a value, but are named with a\0Aleading underscore.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABy convention, fixtures that don't return a value should be named with a\0Aleading underscore, while fixtures that do return a value should not.\0A\0AThis rule ignores abstract fixtures.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture()\0Adef _some_object():\0A    return SomeClass()\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture()\0Adef _some_object_with_cleanup():\0A    obj = SomeClass()\0A    yield obj\0A    obj.cleanup()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture()\0Adef some_object():\0A    return SomeClass()\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture()\0Adef some_object_with_cleanup():\0A    obj = SomeClass()\0A    yield obj\0A    obj.cleanup()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [`pytest` documentation: `@pytest.fixture` functions](https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/reference.html#pytest-fixture)\0A", align 1
@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2074 = private unnamed_addr constant [1054 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `pytest` test functions that should be decorated with\0A`@pytest.mark.usefixtures`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn `pytest`, fixture injection is used to activate fixtures in a test\0Afunction.\0A\0AFixtures can be injected either by passing them as parameters to the test\0Afunction, or by using the `@pytest.mark.usefixtures` decorator.\0A\0AIf the test function depends on the fixture being activated, but does not\0Ause it in the test body or otherwise rely on its return value, prefer\0Athe `@pytest.mark.usefixtures` decorator, to make the dependency explicit\0Aand avoid the confusion caused by unused arguments.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture\0Adef _patch_something(): ...\0A\0A\0Adef test_foo(_patch_something): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0A@pytest.fixture\0Adef _patch_something(): ...\0A\0A\0A@pytest.mark.usefixtures(\22_patch_something\22)\0Adef test_foo(): ...\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [`pytest` documentation: `pytest.mark.usefixtures`](https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/reference.html#pytest-mark-usefixtures)\0A", align 1
