@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2117 = private unnamed_addr constant [1122 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for mocked calls that use a dummy `lambda` function instead of\0A`return_value`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen patching calls, an explicit `return_value` better conveys the intent\0Athan a `lambda` function, assuming the `lambda` does not use the arguments\0Apassed to it.\0A\0A`return_value` is also robust to changes in the patched function's\0Asignature, and enables additional assertions to verify behavior. For\0Aexample, `return_value` allows for verification of the number of calls or\0Athe arguments passed to the patched function via `assert_called_once_with`\0Aand related methods.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef test_foo(mocker):\0A    mocker.patch(\22module.target\22, lambda x, y: 7)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef test_foo(mocker):\0A    mocker.patch(\22module.target\22, return_value=7)\0A\0A    # If the lambda makes use of the arguments, no diagnostic is emitted.\0A    mocker.patch(\22module.other_target\22, lambda x, y: x)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `unittest.mock.patch`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.mock.html#unittest.mock.patch)\0A- [PyPI: `pytest-mock`](https://pypi.org/project/pytest-mock/)\0A", align 1
